[{"type":"thinking","thinking":" Looking at the plugin in `entire.ts`, I can see there's a mismatch with the Plugin API. The `agent.end` handler is registered as a request event, which means it should return an `AgentEndResult` — either `{ action: 'continue', userMessage: string }` or void — but it's currently returning `undefined` without the proper structure.\n\nThe `agent.start` handler also expects an `AgentStartResult` with an optional `message` field, though returning void should be acceptable. There's no error handling or logging when the `entire` binary isn't installed or fails — the plugin silently swallows errors, and the `try/catch` only catches synchronous throws while runtime errors come through the callback. The `cwd` field being passed to the Go subprocess is unused since the Go code doesn't reference it. The plugin correctly handles synthetic `session.start` events via the `seenThreads` tracking, intentionally ignoring the real `session.start` event since `thread.id` may not be present at that point. The `message_id` is properly stringified from the numeric or string `id`, and the `messages` array is cast as `ThreadMessage[]`.\n\nConcurrency is handled safely since `seenThreads` is per-process and the Go agent uses a marker file as a backstop. When writing to stdin via `child.stdin?.end(JSON.stringify(data))`, the data gets written and stdin closes properly since `execFile` pipes stdio by default. The main risk is that large message arrays could exceed the default 1MB stdout buffer, causing an ENOBUFS error that gets silently ignored — but since we don't actually care about the stdout output, this isn't a problem.\n\nAwaiting `fireHook` with its 10-second timeout does add latency to agent handlers, especially for `session.start` before `agent.start`, but it ensures the hooks fire in the correct order. The `seenThreads` map grows unbounded over the plugin's lifetime, which is a minor memory concern. Error handling covers synchronous failures like circular references in `JSON.stringify`, though asynchronous errors from the `execFile` callback are silently ignored. The `status` field matches the Go agent's string format, and the synthetic `session.start` event fires before `agent.start` with both awaited, so they're properly serialized."},{"type":"text","text":"## Review: [.amp/plugins/entire.ts](file:///Users/savekirk/dev/ai/entire/external-agents/.amp/plugins/entire.ts)\n\nThe plugin matches the documented API (`PluginAPI`, `agent.start`, `agent.end`, `helpers.toolCallsInMessages`, `filesModifiedByToolCall`, `filePathFromURI`) and the field names align with [`ampHookPayload` in hooks.go](file:///Users/savekirk/dev/ai/entire/external-agents/agents/entire-agent-amp/internal/amp/hooks.go#L22-L31). Synthesizing `session.start` before the first `agent.start` per thread is correct and matches the AGENT.md design (real `session.start` lacks a guaranteed `thread.id`).\n\n### Issues worth fixing\n\n1. **`maxBuffer` overflow on big turns** — `execFile` defaults to a 1 MB stdout/stderr buffer. The `agent.end` payload includes the full `messages` array; on long turns the child can produce \u003e 1 MB combined or the pipe back-pressure can stall. Even though we ignore stdout, exceeding `maxBuffer` kills the child with `ERR_CHILD_PROCESS_STDIO_MAXBUFFER` and the hook never completes its work. Pass `maxBuffer: Infinity` (or use `spawn` and `stdio: [\"pipe\", \"ignore\", \"ignore\"]`).\n\n2. **Silent failures** — every error path resolves with no log. Use `amp.logger` (available on `PluginAPI`) so users can debug a missing `entire` binary or a non-zero exit:\n   ```ts\n   execFile(\"entire\", […], opts, (err, _stdout, stderr) =\u003e {\n     if (err) amp.logger.log(\"entire hook failed\", hookName, err.message, stderr);\n     resolve();\n   });\n   ```\n\n3. **Synchronous `try/catch` is dead code** — `execFile` reports launch failures via the callback, not by throwing. The `try { … } catch {}` wrapper only catches a `JSON.stringify` failure (circular refs in messages). That is the realistic risk; keep the wrapper but log instead of swallowing.\n\n4. **Handler return types** — `agent.start` and `agent.end` are request events (`PluginRequestResultMap`). Returning `void` is allowed, but TypeScript-strict consumers would flag the implicit `Promise\u003cvoid\u003e`. Annotating the handler `async (event): Promise\u003cvoid\u003e =\u003e {}` makes the intent explicit and prevents accidental return values being misinterpreted later as `{ action: 'continue', … }`.\n\n5. **`as ThreadMessage[]` casts are redundant** — `event.messages` is already `ThreadMessage[]` per `AgentEndEvent`. Removing the casts simplifies and avoids hiding a future type drift.\n\n6. **`seenThreads` grows unbounded** — long-lived plugin processes accumulate IDs forever. Minor, but you already have a filesystem `seen` marker in [hooks.go](file:///Users/savekirk/dev/ai/entire/external-agents/agents/entire-agent-amp/internal/amp/hooks.go#L216-L230) acting as a backstop, so the in-memory set could be dropped or bounded.\n\n7. **Latency on `agent.end`** — awaiting the 10 s `fireHook` delays returning from the request handler, which the docs say can shape further turns (`AgentEndResult`). Fine here because order matters for the Entire transcript, but worth a comment so future maintainers don't \"optimize\" by removing the `await`.\n\n8. **Unused `cwd` field** — the payload sends `cwd: process.cwd()` but [`ampHookPayload`](file:///Users/savekirk/dev/ai/entire/external-agents/agents/entire-agent-amp/internal/amp/hooks.go#L22-L31) reads it but never uses it (the Go side computes paths from `protocol.RepoRoot()`). Either drop it from the plugin or actually consume it (preferring `payload.Cwd` over `RepoRoot()` is safer when Amp launches in a subdirectory).\n\n9. **No subscriptions cleanup** — fine for plugin lifetime, but if you ever add `plugins: reload` re-entry safety, capture the `Subscription` returned by `amp.on(…)`.\n\n### Nits\n\n- The `[item.call, item.result]` loop runs `filesModifiedByToolCall` on both events; per the docstring it accepts either, so this is correct (covers `apply_patch`-style results that only appear after execution). Keep it.\n- `String(event.id)` covers the `ThreadMessageID = number | string` union — good.\n\n### Suggested patch sketch (single block)\n\n```ts\nimport type { PluginAPI } from \"@ampcode/plugin\";\nimport { execFile } from \"node:child_process\";\n\nexport default function (amp: PluginAPI) {\n  const seenThreads = new Set\u003cstring\u003e();\n\n  function fireHook(hookName: string, data: Record\u003cstring, unknown\u003e): Promise\u003cvoid\u003e {\n    return new Promise((resolve) =\u003e {\n      let body: string;\n      try { body = JSON.stringify(data); }\n      catch (err) { amp.logger.log(\"entire hook stringify failed\", hookName, err); return resolve(); }\n\n      const child = execFile(\n        \"entire\",\n        [\"hooks\", \"amp\", hookName],\n        { timeout: 10_000, windowsHide: true, maxBuffer: Infinity },\n        (err, _stdout, stderr) =\u003e {\n          if (err) amp.logger.log(\"entire hook failed\", hookName, err.message, stderr);\n          resolve();\n        },\n      );\n      child.stdin?.end(body);\n    });\n  }\n  // … rest unchanged, drop `as ThreadMessage[]` casts, annotate handlers as Promise\u003cvoid\u003e\n}\n```\n\nThe plugin is structurally sound — these are reliability and observability tightenings rather than correctness fixes."}]}],"timestamp":"2026-05-08T08:59:51Z"}
